ATTENDANCE TRACKER - STEP-BY-STEP BUILD GUIDE
Build it by hand in Excel, starting from a blank workbook.
Excel 2010 or later (desktop). No macros. No Microsoft 365-only functions.
Every formula in this file was tested in a working copy of the tracker.

CONTENTS
  Part 0   Read this first
  Part 1   Create the workbook and the sheets
  Part 2   Named ranges (41 names)
  Part 3   Codes sheet
  Part 4   Setup sheet
  Part 5   Employee Master sheet
  Part 6   Dashboard filter cells (top rows only)
  Part 7   Helper sheet (hidden calculations)
  Part 8   Dropdowns (data validation)
  Part 9   First month tab: Jan-2026
  Part 10  Other month tabs (copies of Jan-2026)
  Part 11  Dashboard: month table, KPI cards, charts
  Part 12  Warning colours on Employee Master, Setup and Codes
  Part 13  Protection and finishing
  Part 14  Test it (with expected results)
  Part 15  Everyday rules and adding a new year
  Appendix A  Colour list (Hex and RGB)
  Appendix B  Troubleshooting


############################################################################
PART 0 - READ THIS FIRST
############################################################################

What you are building
  Dashboard        Year / Month / Team / Sub Team filters, 6 KPI cards,
                   a month-wise table and 2 charts
  Employee Master  ID, Employee Name, Team, Sub Team, Manager, Status (200 rows)
  Setup            Team + Sub Team pairs, holidays, years
  Codes            Attendance codes and what each one counts as
  Helper           Hidden sheet that builds the dropdown lists
  Jan-2026 ...     One tab per month (build Jan-2026 once, then copy it)

How it works, in one paragraph
  Each month tab has one row per employee (row 10 = 1st employee of the Master,
  row 11 = 2nd, and so on) and one column per day (G = day 1 ... AK = day 31).
  You pick a code in each day cell. Columns AL:AQ count each employee's Present,
  Leave, Early Morning, Late Evening, Holiday and Week Off days, using the Codes
  tab to know what each code counts as. The Dashboard finds each month tab by its
  name (e.g. Jan-2026) and adds up those columns for the selected Team / Sub Team.

How to read this guide
  - Every line that STARTS with "=" is a formula. Copy the whole line, click the
    cell named just above it, paste (Ctrl+V) and press Enter.
    In Notepad, turn OFF Format > Word Wrap so each formula stays on one line.
  - "Type" means enter the text or number exactly as shown.
  - Fill down: select the formula cell together with the empty cells below it and
    press Ctrl+D. Fill right: the same across a row, press Ctrl+R.
  - To select a big range fast: click the Name Box (small box left of the formula
    bar), type the range (for example B10:F209) and press Enter.
  - Sheet names, header words marked IMPORTANT and the code words must be spelled
    exactly as shown (spaces and hyphens matter).
  - Formulas use commas between arguments (English Excel). If your Excel uses
    semicolons as the list separator, replace those commas with semicolons.
  - Colours are Hex codes: Fill Color > More Colors > Custom. Excel 2016+ has a
    Hex box; for older versions use the RGB numbers in Appendix A. Plain fill
    colours are cosmetic (skip them if short on time). The colour RULES in
    Part 9.10 are what colour the codes, weekends and holidays.
  - Do the parts in order. The named ranges (Part 2) come early so that every
    formula works the moment you enter it.

Time needed: about 2 to 3 hours.


